<h1>An AI report recommends partnering with Ukrainian IT vendors. Can a buyer act on it?</h1>
<div class="meta">Oleh Demchyna · 14 November 2026 · {{ f.n_claims }} claims checked against their sources · primary data: National Bank of Ukraine</div>
<div class="claimbox"><b>The claim under audit.</b> A deep-research report written by an AI agent (Claude Sonnet 5.5) concludes that "a partnership is viable and commercially sensible, provided it is structured with a Ukraine-plus-EU delivery footprint, continuity clauses, and a focus on senior-heavy, specialized work." It recommends a 3–6 month pilot.</div>
<div class="stats">
  <div><div class="n">{{ f.supported_share }}%</div><div class="l">of the report's {{ f.n_claims }} claims are confirmed by a primary source; {{ f.republisher_share }}% rest only on republishers</div></div>
  <div><div class="n c">{{ f.q5_share }}%</div><div class="l">of claims bear on one question — whether other buyers are staying — which the export data can answer</div></div>
  <div><div class="n a">{{ f.crit_not_full }} of {{ f.crit_n }}</div><div class="l">claims on delivery continuity, cost and contracting are not fully supported ({{ f.crit_wrong }} unsupported or wrong, {{ f.crit_partial }} partly right)</div></div>
</div>
<div class="verdict"><div class="label">Verdict</div>
<p><b>Trust the headline numbers. The pilot is defensible; the case for anything longer is not yet made.</b> The report's headline export figures match central-bank data to within {{ f.maxdev_pp }} percentage points. Its recommended pilot is a sensible first step. But the assumptions a multi-year deal would rest on — delivery continuity, vendor rates, contracting and payment rules — are not evidenced in the report.</p></div>
