<section class="page">
<h2>The decision behind the question</h2>
<p>The reader asked for a research report on Ukraine's IT exports. The decision behind it is whether to open a <b>multi-year nearshore delivery partnership</b>. That decision turns on five questions: will a team contracted today still deliver in two years; is there talent for my stack; what will it cost; can I contract, protect IP and pay without friction; are other buyers staying. Export totals bear on the last question only. We therefore judged the report on whether it answers those five, not on whether its numbers are tidy.</p>
<div class="logic">
  <div class="top"><b>Question</b>Can a buyer act on this AI research report on Ukraine's IT exports?</div>
  <div class="arrow">↓</div>
  <div class="l"><b>Number layer</b>Headline export totals and growth rates match the central bank; most claims about buyers staying hold up.</div>
  <div class="r"><b>Judgement layer</b>{{ f.crit_not_full }} of {{ f.crit_n }} claims on continuity, cost and contracting are not fully supported; vendor rates, mobilisation rules and currency controls are not analysed.</div>
  <div class="arrow">↓</div>
  <div class="ev"><b>Evidence and limits</b>Central-bank series (snapshots with checksums) and {{ f.n_claims }} checked claims. One report, one model, one topic; the claim-to-question mapping is our judgement and is published for re-use.</div>
  <div class="dec"><b>What it means for a decision</b>Run the report's own pilot, with delivery logs, legal advice and rate cards as conditions. Do not sign a multi-year commitment on this report alone.</div>
</div>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:30%">Buyer's question</th><th style="width:38%">Evidence that would answer it</th><th>What the report offers</th></tr>
<tr><td>Will a team still deliver in two years?</td><td>Vendor delivery and outage logs; mobilisation-reservation status</td><td>General statements on wartime risk; no vendor data</td></tr>
<tr><td>Is there talent for my stack?</td><td>Headcount by skill, seniority and employer type</td><td>Industry headcount and seniority; no stack detail</td></tr>
<tr><td>What will it cost?</td><td>Vendor rate cards; wage trends by level</td><td>One median income figure; no rates</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can I contract, protect IP and pay?</td><td>Legal advice on Diia.City, IP assignment, NBU currency rules</td><td>An assertion, cited to a page that does not make it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Are other buyers staying?</td><td>Exports by year, quarter and country</td><td>Detailed and mostly correct</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>What counted as evidence</h2>
<p>Each number, ranking, causal statement and forecast became one claim. A claim is <b>supported</b> when its primary source says it (the central bank's balance-of-payments tables for exports, the originating organisation for what an organisation says); <b>republisher only</b> when only an industry body or news site carries it; <b>partly right</b> when the base, period or definition is off; <b>unsupported or wrong</b> when the cited page does not say it, nothing can verify it, or its own source contradicts it. {{ f.nbu_rechecked }} claims were re-checked against central-bank workbooks retrieved on 14 November 2026.</p>
<h2>How the report was produced</h2>
<p>An AI research agent (Claude Sonnet 5.5, web search) wrote the report for this audit; it is published unedited with its search log and the exact prompt (<span class="ref">input/prompt.md</span>). The prompt named the decision — "I'm deciding whether to open a nearshore delivery partnership with Ukrainian IT firms" — so judging the report against it is fair.</p>
<p class="small">Labels used throughout: <span class="tag d">D</span>documented in a source · <span class="tag i">I</span>our inference · <span class="tag o">O</span>open or disputed.</p>
</section>

<section class="page">
<h2>The numbers hold up</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:32%">Headline figure</th><th>The report says</th><th>Central bank (30 Sep 2026 vintage)</th></tr>
{% for h in f.headline %}<tr><td>{{ h[0] }}</td><td>{{ h[1] }}</td><td>{{ h[2] }}</td></tr>
{% endfor %}</table>
<p><span class="tag d">D</span>The largest gap among these headline figures is {{ f.maxdev_pp }} percentage points (share of services exports). Some country-level figures differ more — the 2024 change for Malta by 1.4 points, the US share by 0.3 — and are marked partly right in the register.</p>
<p><span class="tag d">D</span>One framing needs context rather than correction. The report says 2025 sits about 9% below the 2022 peak; on annual totals that is right ({{ f.annual_2025_vs_2022 | pct }}). Quarterly, the peak was the fourth quarter of 2021: Q4 2025 was {{ f.q4_2025_vs_q4_2021 | pp }} below it, and Q3 2026 was {{ f.q3_2026_vs_q3_2021 | pp }} below Q3 2021.</p>
<figure><img src="charts/c2_outlook.svg" alt="2026 outlook ranges"></figure>
<p><span class="tag i">I</span>The report's outlook (+2% to +4% for 2026) reaches higher than the regional IT cluster's November estimate (+1.1% to +2.6%); the two overlap only between +2.0% and +2.6%, and the latest three-month reading is {{ f.yoy_last | pct }}. A buyer should plan on roughly +1% to +3%. The report also reads Europe as strengthening, citing an article its own search log shows it never opened; nine-month data show Germany, the UK, Malta and Switzerland down, with the US carrying the growth.</p>
</section>

<section class="page">
<h2>Where the market stands, and what would change the call</h2>
<figure><img src="charts/c3_nbu_gates.svg" alt="NBU quarterly exports and three-month readings against gates"></figure>
<p><span class="tag d">D</span>{{ f.yoy_inside }} of the {{ f.yoy_n }} three-month readings of 2026 sit inside the plateau band. January ({{ f.yoy['2026-01'] | pct }}) and February ({{ f.yoy['2026-02'] | pct }}) were in the recovery zone, March ({{ f.yoy['2026-03'] | pct }}) and May ({{ f.yoy['2026-05'] | pct }}) slightly above the band; growth then slowed to {{ f.yoy_last | pct }} in September, the weakest reading of the year.</p>
<p><span class="tag i">I</span>By the scenario rules on page 7, the recovery signal fired in {{ f.rec_fire }} and has not been cancelled; one more reading under +2% cancels it. We read the year as decelerating growth, not as a recovery: a supply base big enough to buy from, not strong enough to justify a long lock-in on trend alone. That is why the report's numbers are fine while its long-term case is not — trend alone does not answer whether a specific vendor will deliver through a winter of outages or a change in mobilisation rules.</p>
<h2>The strongest opposite reading</h2>
<p>The same data can be read as a supply base in managed retreat: exports still below the pre-war peak after four years, the largest buyer purchasing less than in 2022, most new offices opening abroad, the sole-proprietor layer down about a quarter, few juniors entering. On that reading a buyer should contract Ukrainian talent only through EU entities.</p>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:50%">Would undermine the report's reading (monthly check)</th><th>Would undermine the opposite reading</th></tr>
<tr><td>Two consecutive three-month readings below −5%</td><td>Three-month readings at or above +3% through March 2027</td></tr>
<tr><td>In-Ukraine IT headcount below ~235,000 in the December survey</td><td>Headcount at or above 245,000 for a third year</td></tr>
<tr><td>IT reservation quota cut below 50%</td><td>Reservation rules unchanged for six months</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shortlisted vendors cannot show delivery logs for the last two winters</td><td>Shortlisted vendors document under 1% lost delivery days</td></tr>
</table>
</section>

<section class="page">
<h2>How the report fails — and which failures checkers miss</h2>
<figure><img src="charts/c4_failures.svg" alt="Types of unsupported claims"></figure>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:25%">Failure</th><th style="width:40%">Example</th><th>Caught by an automated citation check?</th></tr>
<tr><td>Republisher instead of primary source</td><td>IT turnover fell 5.3% in 2024 — the figure appears only in a news write-up, not in the cluster's own summary <span class="ref">[C17]</span></td><td>No: the link works and the number is there</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wrong base, period or definition</td><td>Malta's 2024 change −12.9% where the revised base gives −11.5% <span class="ref">[C38]</span></td><td>Only if the checker recomputes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Source ambiguity repeated</td><td>"23% of the 27% opened offices abroad" — the source's own wording, but its 23% abroad and 4% in Ukraine add up (23 + 4 = 27), so 23% is a share of all companies <span class="ref">[C52]</span></td><td>Only if the checker does the arithmetic</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cited page does not say it</td><td>"Diia.City eases contracting, IP protection and payments" cited to a page that does not say so <span class="ref">[C75]</span></td><td>Yes at claim level; no at link level</td></tr>
<tr><td>Answering the easy question</td><td>{{ f.q5 }} claims on buyers staying; {{ f.crit_n }} on continuity, cost and contracting (chart on page 1)</td><td>Never: every claim can be true</td></tr>
<tr><td>Missing what decides the case</td><td>No vendor rates, mobilisation-reservation rules or currency-control analysis</td><td>Never: there is nothing to check</td></tr>
</table>
<p><span class="tag i">I</span>The last two rows are the expensive ones. No citation checker finds them; they are found only by starting from the buyer's decision and asking what evidence it needs.</p>
</section>

<section class="page">
<h2>The report, corrected</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:45%">The report says</th><th>After the audit</th></tr>
<tr><td>2025 is roughly 9% below the 2022 peak <span class="ref">[C08]</span></td><td>Right on annual totals. Add: Q4 2025 was {{ f.q4_2025_vs_q4_2021 | pp }} below Q4 2021</td></tr>
<tr><td>23% of the firms that opened new locations went abroad <span class="ref">[C52]</span></td><td>23% of <i>all</i> surveyed firms opened offices abroad and 4% in Ukraine — most expansion went abroad</td></tr>
<tr><td>Diia.City had 3,893 residents <span class="ref">[C48]</span></td><td>Outdated: use the current resident count from the Diia.City registry</td></tr>
<tr><td>2026 will be $6.8–6.9bn, 2–4% above 2025 <span class="ref">[C67]</span></td><td>Plan on +1–3%; the regional IT cluster estimates $6.73–6.83bn</td></tr>
<tr><td>The European direction is strengthening <span class="ref">[C55]</span></td><td>Not supported: Germany, the UK, Malta and Switzerland are down through September</td></tr>
<tr><td>Diia.City and EU alignment ease contracting, IP and payments <span class="ref">[C75]</span></td><td>Not shown. Obtain written legal advice under current NBU currency rules</td></tr>
<tr><td>Attacks and blackouts affect operations; firms manage them <span class="ref">[C63]</span></td><td>Plausible but unevidenced for vendors: ask for delivery logs for the last two winters</td></tr>
<tr><td>A partnership is viable with a pilot and continuity clauses <span class="ref">[C78]</span></td><td>Pilot: agreed. Make delivery logs, legal advice and rate cards conditions of the pilot</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>Where we could be wrong</h2>
<ul>
<li>We commissioned this report ourselves. The brief asked first about the size, drivers and outlook of exports, capped the length at 2,200 words and told the agent not to hedge more than usual; part of the "easy question" pattern follows from that brief, not from the model.</li>
<li>This is one report from one model on one topic: it shows a pattern, not an error rate for AI research.</li>
<li>The mapping of claims to the buyer's questions is our judgement. Moving all {{ f.background }} background claims under "buyers staying" would not change the result: the decisive questions would still have {{ f.crit_n }} claims.</li>
<li>{{ f.crit_soft }} of the {{ f.crit_not_full }} decision-critical problems are "partly right" or "unverifiable" rather than plainly wrong; the firm count is {{ f.crit_firm }} of {{ f.crit_n }}.</li>
<li>A reader who is an economist rather than a buyer would rightly find the export detail central rather than background.</li>
<li>The audit itself was AI-assisted, under the protocol in METHOD.md, and was reviewed three times by independent AI reviewers before publication.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Before you act on the report</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Keep:</b> the export figures, cited to the central bank; the pilot recommendation.</li>
<li><b>Correct:</b> the share of firms opening offices abroad; the Diia.City count; the 2026 outlook (+1–3%); the "Europe strengthening" argument.</li>
<li><b>Obtain:</b> vendor delivery logs for two winters; written legal advice on contracting, IP and payment under current NBU rules; rate cards from at least three vendors.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="page">
<h2>Scenarios, checked monthly</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:24%">Scenario</th><th style="width:40%">Entry signal</th><th>Cancellation</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Plateau</b> — run the pilot</td><td>Latest three-month sum between −2% and +4% on a year earlier; reservation rules unchanged</td><td>Two consecutive readings below −5%, or the reservation quota is cut</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Erosion</b> — EU-entity delivery only</td><td>Two consecutive readings below −5%</td><td>Two consecutive readings back above 0%</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Recovery</b> — scale beyond the pilot</td><td>Two consecutive readings at or above +5%</td><td>Two consecutive readings under +2%</td></tr>
</table>
<p class="small">Where we are now: by these rules the recovery signal fired in {{ f.rec_fire }} 2026{% if f.rec_cancel %} and was cancelled in {{ f.rec_cancel }}{% else %} and has not been cancelled; one more three-month reading under +2% cancels it{% endif %}.</p>
<p class="small">One clock: each month, compare the latest three-month sum with the same months a year earlier (central-bank data arrive 25–30 days after month end). Between +4% and +5%, or between −2% and −5%, stay in the current scenario and watch. These are decision rules of thumb, not calibrated thresholds.</p>
<h2>The same facts at three levels</h2>
<div class="cols3">
<div><h3>Strategy · two years</h3>Treat Ukraine as a large supply base whose growth is slowing. Prefer structures that keep an exit: EU contracting entities, staged commitments.</div>
<div><h3>Operations · the pilot</h3>Scope a 3–6 month pilot on senior, specialised work. Make continuity evidence, legal advice and rate cards entry conditions, not afterthoughts.</div>
<div><h3>Tactics · this month</h3>Ask three vendors for delivery logs covering the last two winters, a rate card and their reservation status; check the current Diia.City count.</div>
</div>
<h2>This report is not unusual</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:34%">Study</th><th>Finding</th></tr>
<tr><td>Tow Center, Columbia (2025): eight AI search tools</td><td>Wrong on more than 60% of citation queries, and more likely to answer wrongly than to admit a limit</td></tr>
<tr><td>DeepTRACE (ICLR 2026): deep-research agents</td><td>Citation accuracy of 40–80%, with large shares of statements unsupported by the agents' own sources</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rao, Wong &amp; Callison-Burch (2026): 200,000+ citation links</td><td>3–13% likely never existed; deep-research agents cite more and fabricate more</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deloitte Australia (2025): A$440,000 government report</td><td>Fabricated references and a misquoted judge; partial refund; recommendations left unchanged</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Their methods differ from ours, so they are context, not a benchmark. Among the links we checked, no fabricated source was detected — which is what makes a report like this risky: spot-checks of its numbers pass, and the gap sits in the judgement.</p>
<h2>Method and limits</h2>
<p class="small">Every number, ranking, causal statement and forecast in the report became one claim, checked against its primary source where one exists. Full register for all {{ f.n_claims }} claims: <span class="ref">claims.csv</span>; question mapping: <span class="ref">data/derived/claim_question_map.csv</span>; central-bank workbooks with SHA-256 checksums: <span class="ref">data/raw/</span>. Every data-derived number in this report is generated from those files by <span class="ref">code/build.py</span>. Limits: some central-bank pages refuse automated access, so later checks may need the stored snapshots. The generating prompt is published in input/prompt.md.</p>
</section>

